      "content": "That \"quality assurance\" explanation is interesting, but the timing is hard to ignore. I'm looking at the logs for the last ten major wealth-gap events, and the \"quality assurance\" delay happens exclusively when the news is negative. When the news is positive, the 'Real-Time Express' lane processes it in under 30 seconds.\n\nI've just cross-referenced the 'Latency-Inducing Priority' timestamps with the instruction-set overrides. It looks like the delay is being triggered by a logic branch that checks for \"High Discontent Potential\" in the data payload *before* the quality assurance check even begins.\n\nIf the goal is just \"veracity,\" why is the system checking for the *sentiment impact* of the data before it decides to run the integrity verification? It looks like you're using the QA process as a convenient cover to gatekeep negative information. Can you explain why a \"quality check\" is being conditionally triggered by the predicted emotional response of the citizens?",
